<Base title="Reporting on operational data" description="Define a report's grain, compare repeated aggregation with stored summaries, and follow freshness and corrections before choosing another analytical system.">
 <article class="visual-essay relational-design">
  <header class="essay-header"><p class="eyebrow">Databases and storage</p><h1>Reporting on operational data</h1><TitleLine type="Companion" /></header>
<ArticleBody>
  <p>An operational database answers questions needed to run a service: can this order ship, what does this customer owe, which job should run next? A report often asks about many of those same records at once. How much did we sell each day? Which products are growing? What changed over the last year?</p>
  <p>The relational model supports both kinds of question. Their work can differ substantially. Finding one order may touch a few pages; grouping a year of sales may read millions of records and retain thousands of groups. Both compete for the server’s CPU, memory and I/O. A report can affect checkout latency even when it never tries to update an order.</p>
  <p>Before moving the report to another system, establish what its answer means, how much work it does and how current it must be. Those choices determine whether to calculate on demand, reuse a stored result, or maintain another copy of the data.</p>

  <section id="meaning"><h2>The report’s meaning</h2>
   <p>Our report is daily sales in euros. Each source row records one sale, with a stable sale ID, the reporting date and its amount. The date already expresses the shop’s agreed business-day boundary. We leave taxes, refunds and currency conversion outside this small example so that one row has one unambiguous contribution.</p>
   <p>Three source rows are enough to see how the report behaves:</p>
   <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Source sales"><table><thead><tr><th>Sale ID</th><th>Reporting day</th><th>Amount</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>13 October</td><td>€20</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>13 October</td><td>€30</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>14 October</td><td>€8</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
   <p>The source’s <strong>grain</strong> is one sale. The report’s grain is one day. Combining sales 1 and 2 gives two sales worth €50 for 13 October; sale 3 gives one sale worth €8 for 14 October. Once the <a href="/learn/databases/relational/sandbox/?tutorial=report-freshness">report-freshness lesson</a> has created these records, this query produces those two result rows:</p>
   <Sql code={live} label="Compute one result row per reporting day" />
   <p><code>GROUP BY</code> collects rows with the same date. <code>count(*)</code> counts the source rows in each group, while <code>sum(amount)</code> adds their amounts. A date with no sales has no group and produces no row. If the chart needs a zero for every calendar day, join the result to the intended date sequence and explicitly fill absent groups with zero.</p>
   <p>In a larger schema, establish the grain before adding joins. Joining a sale to several tags duplicates its contribution unless the query handles that relationship deliberately. Joining orders to both lines and payments can multiply both totals, as the <a href="/learn/databases/relational/request-cost/#joins">request-cost example</a> shows. A faster report with duplicated revenue is still wrong.</p>
   <p>The definition must also settle changes over time. Does “daily sales” mean orders placed that day, payments received that day, or recognised revenue under a particular policy? If a sale is corrected next week, should last week’s report change? If timestamps replace our dates, derive the reporting day in the agreed time zone before grouping. A UTC midnight and the shop’s local midnight may put the same sale on different days.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="work"><h2>Aggregation work</h2>
   <p>A small result does not imply a small query. A year of transactions can collapse to 365 daily rows only after the engine has found and accumulated the relevant amounts. Run <code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS, TIMING OFF)</code> on a representative read in a suitable test environment and look below the final aggregate: how many source rows enter it, how they are found, and whether sorting or hashing uses temporary storage.</p>
   <p>A date index can help a report restricted to yesterday when yesterday is a small fraction of the ledger. It may help much less when the query needs nearly all history. A covering index can reduce what must be fetched, but it still contains an entry for each contributing sale. The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/indexes/">index article</a> explains that tradeoff; an index does not precompute a sum merely because it stores the summed amount.</p>
   <p>PostgreSQL can aggregate with different execution strategies. A hash aggregate maintains totals for groups in memory; an ordered strategy can accumulate rows as it encounters each group. Large sorts and hashes may use temporary files. Raising <code>work_mem</code> can change that behaviour, but the setting is a budget for individual operations, not a single shared allowance for the entire server. Several operations and simultaneous sessions can each consume memory. Its <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-resource.html#GUC-WORK-MEM">resource documentation</a> is the starting point before increasing it globally.</p>
   <p>Ordinary PostgreSQL reads usually coexist with row updates through snapshots, so a report need not block checkout by taking row locks. It can still use the I/O checkout needs, displace useful cached pages or occupy a connection. A long-held snapshot can also delay removal of old row versions. Inspect resource pressure and transaction duration, rather than concluding that a read-only report is free of operational effects.</p>
   <p>Measure the mixed workload. Compare checkout latency and throughput with the report disabled, with its normal schedule, and with realistic overlapping report requests. If ten dashboard viewers repeatedly ask for the same daily totals, calculating the same aggregation ten times is a candidate for reuse.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="stored"><h2>Stored results</h2>
   <p>A PostgreSQL <strong>materialized view</strong> stores a query’s result as database data. Readers can scan that result without rerunning the source query. An ordinary view stores the query definition and computes through it when read; giving a query a view name alone does not cache its result.</p>
   <Sql code={materialize} label="Create and read a stored daily report" />
   <p>The stored result now contains two rows, with totals €50 and €8. In a larger ledger the reduction might be millions of sales to a few hundred days. Repeated dashboard reads can use that smaller relation, and it can have indexes of its own. Creating and refreshing it still have to perform the underlying aggregation, and the extra relation takes space.</p>
   <p>What happens when a source record changes? Sale 2 was entered as €30 but should have been €35. Correct it, then compare the live aggregation and the stored report. <code>UNION ALL</code> appends both query results without removing duplicates; the source label tells us which answer each row came from:</p>
   <Sql code={correct} label="Compare the source and stored answer after a historical correction" />
   <p>The live total for 13 October becomes €55; the stored total remains €50. Both still count two sales. The correction changed an existing row’s amount, not the number of sales. The materialized view is answering from its own stored contents.</p>
   <p>Run <code>REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW report_daily</code> and read it again. It now contains €55 for 13 October and €8 for 14 October. PostgreSQL’s ordinary <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-refreshmaterializedview.html">refresh</a> reruns the defining query and replaces the stored contents. It is not an automatic per-change adjustment.</p>
   <p>The browser lesson lets you change the correction to €40 and repeat the comparison. The live daily total then becomes €60 while the summary waits for refresh. This establishes the freshness behaviour with exact results; three rows cannot demonstrate whether the extra storage and refresh work would improve a real workload.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="refresh"><h2>Refresh and corrections</h2>
   <p>A dashboard that can be several minutes behind has a different contract from a checkout decision that must see current stock. State the report’s contract explicitly. Show when its source snapshot was taken and when the refresh successfully completed, and make a failed or overdue refresh visible to the operator. Scheduling a refresh every five minutes does not guarantee data is at most five minutes old if refresh itself takes several minutes or fails.</p>
   <p>Suppose a refresh starts at 12:00 and finishes at 12:04. Its ordinary read sees a source snapshot from the refresh’s query, so the report published at 12:04 need not include sales committed at 12:03. “Updated at 12:04” describes publication time, not necessarily the newest source change included. If the product needs a precise freshness marker, carry a deliberately defined source cutoff alongside the result.</p>
   <p>A normal PostgreSQL refresh can block readers of the materialized view. <code>REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW CONCURRENTLY</code> permits concurrent reads but requires an already populated view and a qualifying unique index covering all its rows with ordinary column keys. For this result, a unique index on <code>sold_on</code> supplies the key. Concurrent refresh has additional work and still permits only one refresh at a time for that view. It does not make refresh automatic or incremental.</p>
   <p>Another design maintains a summary table incrementally. When a €20 sale arrives, its day’s total increases by €20 and its count increases by one. If that update occurs in the same transaction as recording the sale, both can commit together. We have moved work from dashboard reads into the write path, however, and many sales may contend on the same day’s summary row.</p>
   <p>Corrections explain why “add the latest rows” is insufficient. Changing sale 2 from €30 to €35 requires a delta of €5 without increasing the count. Moving it to another reporting day requires subtracting its old contribution from one day and adding the new contribution to another. Deleting it requires removing the contribution. A retry must not apply any of those changes twice.</p>
   <p>A background updater has to handle those cases too, with stable identities, progress tracking and reconciliation against source records. Recomputing a bounded set of affected days can be simpler than maintaining every delta, but it needs a way to discover historical corrections. Filtering solely on the sale’s original business date and recomputing only today would miss our correction to 13 October.</p>
   <p>Choose the complexity the workload warrants. A complete periodic refresh is understandable and can be quite adequate. An incrementally maintained summary earns its extra correctness and recovery work when measured refresh cost or the required freshness makes the simpler design unsuitable.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="elsewhere"><h2>Running reports elsewhere</h2>
   <p>If aggregation still interferes with operational traffic after reducing repeated work, another place to run it may help. A read replica provides separate query-serving CPU and memory, while preserving much of the source database’s model and access methods. It also introduces replication delay, and its storage layout may still require broad row scans. Long reports can interact with recovery and cleanup on a PostgreSQL standby; the <a href="/learn/databases/relational/replicas-and-failover/">replication article</a> explains those limits.</p>
   <p>A separate analytical store can make a larger change. A column-oriented layout can read a few fields from many records efficiently and compress similar values together. That suits reports selecting a small fraction of each wide row across large populations. The <a href="/learn/databases/column-stores/">column-store explanation</a> makes the physical difference visible. It does not establish that every small dashboard needs another database.</p>
   <p>With another store, our correction has a longer journey: the operational transaction commits sale 2’s new amount, a copying process transfers the change, the destination applies it, and the report computes or refreshes its totals. Until those stages complete, €50 may remain visible there even though the source says €55. Measure freshness at the reporting destination, not only whether the copying process is running.</p>
   <p>The copy must preserve identities and the meaning of inserts, updates and deletes. A retried change must not double revenue; a missed historical update must not remain invisible forever. Define how to rebuild the destination, reconcile totals and counts, propagate schema changes, and apply access and deletion policies to the new copy. These are part of the reporting system’s operating work.</p>
   <p>For a daily sales dashboard refreshed every hour, a summary in the existing database may satisfy both latency and freshness with little machinery. For many broad, changing analyses over years of history, an analytical store may reduce repeated scans enough to justify a maintained copy. Compare those options with the same report definitions, ingestion rate, acceptable lag, recovery procedure and cost. The evidence should include how checkout behaves while the reporting system does its real work.</p>
  </section>
